is to discard a tile that is not part of any potential meld. For example, J3. \\n\\nAlternatively, if I discard T6, I can form a T6-7-8 Chow, which is a good meld. That would help me form more melds. \\n\\nSo if I discard T6, then I can form a concealed T6-7-8 Chow. That's a good move. \\n\\nAfter that, I have T7, T8, T9 left. Maybe form a T7-8-9 Chow. Then, the remaining tiles would be W1, W2, T1, T2, T2, B9, J3. \\n\\nWith T2, T2 as a pair, then I need another meld. W1 and W2 could be part of a 1-2-3 Chow if I get a W3. But I don't have it. \\n\\nAlternatively, maybe there's a Pung of T2. But I only have two. \\n\\nThis is tricky. Maybe the best option is to discard T6 to form the T6-7-8 Chow, even if it leaves some uncertainty. \\n\\nAlternatively, discard T9. Then, I can form T6-7-8 and maybe T1-2-3. \\n\\nBut I have T1 and T2. If I discard T9, then I have T6, T7, T8 which can form a Chow. \\n\\nSo, the best move seems to be to discard T6 to form a T6-7-8 Chow. That would be a good meld. \\n\\nAlternatively, discard T2 to form a T1-2-3 Chow if possible. \\n\\nBut I have two T2s. If I discard one T2, I can keep one T2. Then, if I get a T3, I can form a T1-2-3 Chow. \\n\\nBut the risk is that someone might Chi the T2. \\n\\nConsidering the valid actions, the options are Play T6, T8, T9, etc. \\n\\nDiscarding T6 seems to be a safe move to form a concealed Chow. \\n\\nSo, the answer would be Play T6.\\n\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: Discarding T6 allows forming a concealed Chow of T6-7-8, which contributes to a structured hand with multiple melds and improves scoring potential.  \\nReason: Forming a concealed Chow of T6-7-8 creates a clear path to completing additional melds (e.g., T7-8-9) and secures a pair (T2, T2). This move reduces uncertainty and aligns with high-scoring patterns like Full Flush or Pure Straight.  \\nAnswer: Play T6\"}"}
