uce the number of non-terminal tiles. For example, playing B8 removes one non-terminal. Then, playing T2 or T7 or T8 would help. \n\nBut since they can only discard one tile now, which one is the best to discard? \n\nIf they discard B8, they have one less non-terminal. Then, they can later discard others. Alternatively, if they discard T2, they lose the chance to form T1-T2-T3 chow but reduce non-terminals. \n\nBut the user's hand already has a chow in W4-6 and possibly T7-9. If they focus on forming two chows and a pair, maybe they can get a different pattern. \n\nAlternatively, if they discard W2, which is a simple tile not part of any chow. \n\nLooking at the valid actions, the user can choose to play any of the listed tiles. Which one is the best to discard to maximize their chances of completing a winning hand? \n\nConsidering the All Terminals and Honors possibility, discarding B8 would help. B8 is a Dot 8, not a terminal or honor. So playing B8 would reduce the non-terminal count. Then, the user can later discard T2, T7, or T8. \n\nAlternatively, if they discard T2, they can work on the T1-T3 chow. \n\nBut the user has to choose one tile now. \n\nAnother thought: the user's hand has a pair of W1. If they can form a hand with two pairs and three chows, but that's not standard. The standard is four melds and a pair. \n\nGiven the current hand, the best move might be to discard a tile that is not part of any potential meld. B8 is a non-terminal and not part of any chow. Discarding B8 would help in moving towards All Terminals and Honors. \n\nAlternatively, if they discard T2, they might lose the T1-T2-T3 possibility, but if they can get a T3, that's a chow. But if they keep T2, they can try to get T3. \n\nBut the user has to decide now. Considering the unshown tiles, T3 is available (4 copies left). So if they keep T1 and T2, they can try to draw T3. \n\nHowever, the user's current hand has a lot of non-terminal tiles. Discarding B8 seems like a good move to reduce the non-terminals. \n\nSo the answer is to play B8.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has a meld of W4-W6 and several terminals and honors. Discarding B8 (a non-terminal) helps progress towards All Terminals and Honors (32 points), while retaining T1/T2 for potential T1-T2-T3 chow and T7-T8-T9 chow.  \nReason: B8 is a non-terminal/honor tile, and discarding it reduces the hand's non-terminal count, aligning with high-value patterns like All Terminals and Honors. Keeping T1/T2 preserves chances for additional chows.  \nAnswer: Play B8"}
